1
00:00:00,161 --> 00:00:04,944
Imagine giving an engineer a short request, and also
handing them your team's engineering

2
00:00:04,944 --> 00:00:09,726
handbook. That is a useful way to understand poteto
mode. In this film, we

3
00:00:09,726 --> 00:00:14,508
will unpack Lauren Tan's skill file, then follow an
illustrated Portage feature from a

4
00:00:14,508 --> 00:00:16,899
user's intention to evidence that it works.

5
00:00:19,006 --> 00:00:24,222
The file describes Lauren's preferred engineering
style. Read the system before changing it. Choose

6
00:00:24,222 --> 00:00:29,438
clear structures. Keep the code and explanation
simple. Delegate deliberately, and verify the
outcome.

7
00:00:29,438 --> 00:00:34,653
My interpretation is that she wants these habits to
become repeatable, so an agent

8
00:00:34,653 --> 00:00:39,869
can carry more of the engineering work while leaving
evidence that a person can

9
00:00:39,869 --> 00:00:40,242
review.

10
00:00:41,906 --> 00:00:47,468
Open skill dot M D and you will find several layers.
Metadata identifies the

11
00:00:47,468 --> 00:00:53,031
skill. Triggers connect situations to other skills.
Principles guide decisions. Playbooks organize
different tasks,

12
00:00:53,031 --> 00:00:58,593
such as features, bug fixes, and investigations.
This is instruction text read by an

13
00:00:58,593 --> 00:01:04,156
agent. It is not an executable engine that
automatically forces every step to happen.

14
00:01:05,842 --> 00:01:10,782
Suppose you ask, use poteto mode to add OAuth for
remote MCP servers in

15
00:01:10,782 --> 00:01:15,722
Portage. The agent recognizes a feature request and
reads the feature playbook. A broken

16
00:01:15,722 --> 00:01:20,661
connection would instead call for a bug fix. A
question about how authentication works

17
00:01:20,661 --> 00:01:25,601
would call for an investigation. The mode
coordinates the relevant workflow. It does not

18
00:01:25,601 --> 00:01:27,718
run every skill for every request.

19
00:01:29,341 --> 00:01:34,749
The how skill traces what the system actually does.
For Portage, that means following

20
00:01:34,749 --> 00:01:40,158
settings, credential storage, MCP connections, and
Sora tool calls. The why skill asks what

21
00:01:40,158 --> 00:01:45,566
led to that design, using history and available
records. Why becomes especially relevant when

22
00:01:45,566 --> 00:01:50,975
ownership or layering changes. If the evidence is
missing, the agent should say so

23
00:01:50,975 --> 00:01:53,293
rather than inventing the original intention.

24
00:01:54,929 --> 00:02:00,824
Next comes architecture. The architect skill uses
arena to explore different designs and synthesize

25
00:02:00,824 --> 00:02:06,718
a choice. For OAuth, an illustrative comparison is
SDK managed authentication versus application
managed

26
00:02:06,718 --> 00:02:12,613
authentication. The agent should investigate actual
support before choosing. The feature workflow then
breaks

27
00:02:12,613 --> 00:02:18,508
delivery into verifiable units. A separate plan is
not a universal skill. If you

28
00:02:18,508 --> 00:02:22,718
want to approve the design first, explicitly request
that checkpoint.

29
00:02:24,357 --> 00:02:29,909
One of the file's recurring ideas is to model the
domain before filling in

30
00:02:29,909 --> 00:02:35,460
logic. In an OAuth example, disconnected,
authorizing, connected, refreshing, and reconnect
required are meaningful

31
00:02:35,460 --> 00:02:41,011
states. Making those transitions explicit can be
clearer than scattering unrelated flags across the

32
00:02:41,011 --> 00:02:46,562
code. This diagram illustrates the principle. It
does not claim that OAuth has already

33
00:02:46,562 --> 00:02:48,148
been implemented in Portage.

34
00:02:49,796 --> 00:02:55,109
Poteto mode asks the lead agent to delegate focused
work, then own the result.

35
00:02:55,109 --> 00:03:00,422
Investigators can inspect different areas. An
implementation agent can work within a defined
scope.

36
00:03:00,422 --> 00:03:05,735
A reviewer can challenge the result. Independent
work may run in parallel, but shared

37
00:03:05,735 --> 00:03:11,047
changes need coordination. The exact agent types and
model choices depend on the host.

38
00:03:11,047 --> 00:03:15,222
Installing the skill cannot create capabilities that
Codex does not expose.

39
00:03:16,844 --> 00:03:22,624
Verification closes the loop. For Portage, our
verify Portage driver launches an isolated instance,

40
00:03:22,624 --> 00:03:28,403
exercises behavior, and saves evidence. A successful
connection is different from a successful tool

41
00:03:28,403 --> 00:03:34,183
call. A successful tool call is different from a
correct answer. Reports should distinguish

42
00:03:34,183 --> 00:03:39,963
passed, failed, blocked, and review required. A
green build alone cannot prove the complete

43
00:03:39,963 --> 00:03:40,788
user journey.

44
00:03:42,456 --> 00:03:48,469
The autonomy section encourages progress on
authorized, reversible work. Its always pause
section names

45
00:03:48,469 --> 00:03:54,483
consequential actions, including deployments, data
deletion, customer messages, and force pushes to
shared branches.

46
00:03:54,483 --> 00:04:00,497
Read these as workflow instructions within the
host's actual permissions and your authorization.
The

47
00:04:00,497 --> 00:04:06,510
skill cannot override those boundaries. Ask for an
additional design checkpoint whenever you want

48
00:04:06,510 --> 00:04:09,087
to review a choice before implementation.

49
00:04:10,743 --> 00:04:15,685
How do you know the agent followed the workflow?
Inspect the skill files it

50
00:04:15,685 --> 00:04:20,626
read, the tasks it delegated, the commands it ran,
and the artifacts it produced.

51
00:04:20,626 --> 00:04:25,568
For long work, show me your work adds a decision
trail. These are observable

52
00:04:25,568 --> 00:04:30,509
records, not access to private internal reasoning.
If a tool, model, or workflow step

53
00:04:30,509 --> 00:04:33,333
is unavailable, the agent should disclose that
limitation.

54
00:04:34,944 --> 00:04:39,707
Your part is to state the goal, the finish line, the
evidence, and the

55
00:04:39,707 --> 00:04:44,470
real constraints. For example, let users authorize a
remote MCP server, then prove an

56
00:04:44,470 --> 00:04:49,233
authenticated tool call succeeds. Keep credentials
out of logs, and let me review the

57
00:04:49,233 --> 00:04:53,996
design first. You do not need to prescribe every
skill in sequence. Clear intent

58
00:04:53,996 --> 00:04:56,717
gives the playbook room to do its job.

59
00:04:58,382 --> 00:05:04,063
My reading of Lauren's intent is to turn strong
engineering habits into reusable instructions,

60
00:05:04,063 --> 00:05:09,745
supported by tools that can prove outcomes. The
human provides direction. The agent investigates,

61
00:05:09,745 --> 00:05:15,426
designs, implements, and verifies. Poteto mode
supplies the playbook. The host supplies the
capabilities.

62
00:05:15,426 --> 00:05:21,108
The evidence lets you assess the result. That is the
useful idea to bring

63
00:05:21,108 --> 00:05:21,919
into Portage.
